                        <p>Belsham began his literary career with <title>Essays, Philosophical, Historical and Literary</title> (1789) which were well recieved. He followed this with 
    a second collection of essays which again demonstrated his dissenting and reforming principles, including an attack on 
      <persName key="BUR10">Edmund Burke</persName>. These views were shared 
    with his brother <persName key="BEL01">Thomas</persName> who was a significant Unitarian preacher. Much of his later life was dedicated to history writing.</p>
